= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <group representation> on a <vector space> $V$ is a <group homomorphism> $R:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V)$, so $R(gh)=R(g)R(h)$ and $R(e)=I$. A nonzero <group representation> is an <irreducible representation> if its only <invariant subspaces> are $0$ and $V$. Here the <representations> and <characters> are finite-dimensional over the <complex numbers>.

The <character of a representation> is $\chi_R(g)=\operatorname{tr}R(g)$. Three useful properties are:

* It is a <class function>: $\chi_R(hgh^{-1})=\chi_R(g)$, by the cyclic property of the <trace>.
* It is additive on a <direct sum> and multiplicative on a <tensor product of group representations>: $\chi_{R\oplus S}=\chi_R+\chi_S$ and $\chi_{R\otimes S}=\chi_R\chi_S$.
* The <irreducible characters> obey <character orthogonality>:
  $$
  \langle\chi_r,\chi_s\rangle=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}\chi_r(g)^*\chi_s(g)=\delta_{rs}.
  $$

In particular, $\chi_R(e)=\dim V$ and $\chi_R(g^{-1})=\chi_R(g)^*$, the latter following from <unitarization of a finite-group representation>. The <character inner product> $\langle\chi_r,\chi_R\rangle$ counts the multiplicity of an <irreducible representation> $r$ in $R$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For any $h\in G$, left multiplication permutes the elements of the <finite group>. Consequently <averaging over a finite group> gives
$$
R(h)Pv=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}R(hg)v=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{k\in G}R(k)v=Pv.
$$
Thus $Pv$ belongs to the <fixed-point subspace of a group action> $V^G$; any nonzero such vector spans a <trivial representation>, or singlet. The zero vector is included in the invariance statement.

Conversely $Pw=w$ for every $w\in V^G$, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{im}P=V^G,\qquad P^2=P.}
$$
This is a <projection operator> onto all singlets, rather than necessarily onto a one-dimensional <vector subspace>. Its <trace> is the singlet multiplicity $|G|^{-1}\sum_g\chi_R(g)$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $r=g_1$ and $s=g_2$. Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
r^3=s^2=I,\qquad srs=r^{-1}.
$$
Moving every $s$ to the right shows that every word in these <generators of a group> has the form $r^a s^b$, with $a\in\{0,1,2\}$ and $b\in\{0,1\}$. The three $r^a$ are distinct diagonal <matrices>, and the three $r^as$ are distinct off-diagonal <matrices>. Hence
$$
\boxed{G=\{I,r,r^2,s,rs,r^2s\},\qquad |G|=6.}
$$
Since $srs=r^2\ne r$, this is a <non-Abelian group>. Its <group presentation> identifies it with the <dihedral group> of an equilateral triangle, equivalently the <symmetric group> $S_3$.

The <conjugacy classes> are
$$
\boxed{\{I\},\qquad \{r,r^2\},\qquad \{s,rs,r^2s\}.}
$$
Indeed $s$ interchanges $r$ and $r^2$ by <conjugation>, while $r^a s r^{-a}=r^{2a}s$ runs through all three reflections. No reflection is conjugate to a rotation, since <conjugation> preserves <order of a group element>.

There are three inequivalent complex <irreducible representations>, one for each <conjugacy class>. The <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions> gives $d_1^2+d_2^2+d_3^2=6$. One <representation> is trivial, and the only possible positive integer dimensions are
$$
\boxed{d_1,d_2,d_3=1,1,2.}
$$

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write $\mathbf1$ for the <trivial representation>, $\varepsilon$ for the <sign representation>, and $\mathbf2$ for the given two-dimensional <group representation>. Their <matrix representations> on the <generators of a group> are
$$
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 &r&s\\\hline
\mathbf1&1&1\\
\varepsilon&1&-1\\
\mathbf2&\operatorname{diag}(\alpha,\alpha^{-1})&\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}
\end{array}
$$
with $\alpha=e^{2\pi i/3}$. The two <eigenvalues> of $r$ in $\mathbf2$ are distinct, and $s$ exchanges its two <eigenlines>; no line is invariant under both. Thus $\mathbf2$ is an <irreducible representation>. The dimensions already exhaust the <sum of squares of irreducible representation dimensions>, so this is the complete list.

Since $\alpha+\alpha^{-1}=-1$, the <character table> is
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|rrr}
\text{class}&I&\{r,r^2\}&\{s,rs,r^2s\}\\
\text{class size}&1&2&3\\\hline
\chi_{\mathbf1}&1&1&1\\
\chi_{\varepsilon}&1&1&-1\\
\chi_{\mathbf2}&2&-1&0
\end{array}.}
$$
Multiplying <characters> and using <character orthogonality> gives every <tensor product of group representations>:
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
\otimes&\mathbf1&\varepsilon&\mathbf2\\\hline
\mathbf1&\mathbf1&\varepsilon&\mathbf2\\
\varepsilon&\varepsilon&\mathbf1&\mathbf2\\
\mathbf2&\mathbf2&\mathbf2&\mathbf1\oplus\varepsilon\oplus\mathbf2
\end{array}.}
$$
For example $\chi_{\mathbf2}^2=(4,1,0)=\chi_{\mathbf1}+\chi_\varepsilon+\chi_{\mathbf2}$. Explicit <Clebsch-Gordan coefficients> can be read from two doublets $x=(x_1,x_2)^T$, $y=(y_1,y_2)^T$:
$$
\mathbf1:\ x_1y_2+x_2y_1,\qquad
\varepsilon:\ x_1y_2-x_2y_1,\qquad
\mathbf2:\ \begin{pmatrix}x_2y_2\\x_1y_1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The first two combinations are invariant under $r$ and respectively even and odd under $s$; the last transforms by the displayed doublet <matrices>.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Choose the doublet <bases> so that the column of barred fields $B=(\bar\psi_L^1,\bar\psi_L^2)^T$ and $R=(\psi_R^1,\psi_R^2)^T$ both transform by the doublet <matrices> in <1/d>. This choice follows the source's assignment of the barred doublet itself. Since $H$ transforms in $\varepsilon$, its <Yukawa coupling> must multiply a fermion bilinear in $\varepsilon$.

There is exactly one such bilinear in $\mathbf2\otimes\mathbf2$: the <Clebsch-Gordan coefficients> in <1/d> give $B_1R_2-B_2R_1$. Equivalently, writing the upper block of the <Yukawa matrix> as $A$, invariance requires
$$
D(r)^TAD(r)=A,\qquad D(s)^TAD(s)=-A.
$$
The first equation sets $A_{11}=A_{22}=0$, because $\alpha^2\ne1$; the second sets $A_{21}=-A_{12}$.

A mixed doublet-singlet bilinear remains a doublet after multiplication by $H$, so all four mixed entries vanish. Finally $\bar\psi_L^3\psi_R^3$ transforms in $\varepsilon\otimes\mathbf1=\varepsilon$, and multiplication by $H$ makes it invariant. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\begin{pmatrix}0&a&0\\-a&0&0\\0&0&b\end{pmatrix},\qquad a,b\in\mathbb C.}
$$
The allowed <Yukawa interaction> is $H\{a(\bar\psi_L^1\psi_R^2-\bar\psi_L^2\psi_R^1)+b\bar\psi_L^3\psi_R^3\}$, together with its <Hermitian conjugate> when forming a real <Lagrangian density>.

The displayed texture depends on <basis>. If instead the unbarred left doublet is assigned $D$ in the same ordering as the right doublet, its barred doublet has the <dual representation> $D^*$ and the equivalent upper block is $a\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$. Exchanging the two barred components converts between these descriptions. In either convention there are exactly two independent complex <Yukawa couplings>.

