= Solution
{parent=a}

For a curve $O(t)=I+tX+O(t^2)$ in the <special orthogonal group>, differentiate $O(t)^TO(t)=I$ at the identity. This gives $X^T+X=0$. Conversely, the <matrix exponential> of any real <skew-symmetric matrix> is orthogonal and has <determinant> $\det e^{tX}=e^{t\operatorname{tr}X}=1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak{so}(N)=\{X\in M_N(\mathbb R):X^T=-X\},\qquad [X,Y]=XY-YX.}
$$
A <basis> is $L_{ij}=E_{ij}-E_{ji}$ for $1\le i<j\le N$, where $E_{ij}$ is a <matrix unit>. If $L_{ji}=-L_{ij}$ and $L_{ii}=0$, their <Lie brackets> are
$$
[L_{ij},L_{kl}]=\delta_{jk}L_{il}-\delta_{jl}L_{ik}-\delta_{ik}L_{jl}+\delta_{il}L_{jk}.
$$
There are $\binom N2$ independent off-diagonal entries. A <maximal torus> rotates independently in mutually orthogonal two-planes, generated by $L_{12},L_{34},\ldots$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\dim\mathfrak{so}(N)=\frac{N(N-1)}2,\qquad \operatorname{rank}\operatorname{SO}(N)=\left\lfloor\frac N2\right\rfloor.}
$$
The maximality follows by simultaneously decomposing commuting real <skew-symmetric matrices> into rotation two-planes, with at most one remaining fixed axis.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The unbroken <group> is the <stabilizer subgroup> of the <vacuum expectation value>. For a nonzero vector <vacuum expectation value>, an <orthogonal transformation> puts $\langle\phi\rangle=v e_N$. A special orthogonal transformation fixing it has the block form $\operatorname{diag}(A,1)$ with $A\in\operatorname{SO}(N-1)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{SO}(N)\longrightarrow\operatorname{SO}(N-1)\quad(v\ne0).}
$$
The zero <vacuum expectation value> leaves the entire <special orthogonal group> unbroken.

For a real <symmetric tensor> write its <vacuum expectation value> as a real <symmetric matrix> $Q$. The transformation is $Q\mapsto OQO^T$. The <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> gives an orthonormal <basis> in which
$$
Q=\operatorname{diag}(q_1I_{m_1},\ldots,q_pI_{m_p}),\qquad q_i\ne q_j\ (i\ne j),\qquad \sum_i m_i=N.
$$
Because $OQO^T=Q$ is equivalent to $OQ=QO$, an unbroken transformation preserves each <eigenspace>. The complete unbroken <group> is therefore
$$
\boxed{H=S\bigl(O(m_1)\times\cdots\times O(m_p)\bigr)
=\{(A_1,\ldots,A_p):\prod_i\det A_i=1\}.}
$$
Its <identity component> is $H^0=\prod_i\operatorname{SO}(m_i)$. This <symmetric tensor vacuum stabilizer> shows why the <representation> alone does not determine a unique breaking pattern:

* If $Q=qI$, all of $\operatorname{SO}(N)$ survives, even when $q\ne0$.
* If all <eigenvalues> are distinct, $H\cong(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)^{N-1}$, consisting of diagonal sign changes with total sign $+1$; no continuous symmetry survives.
* If the two <eigenvalue multiplicities> are $m$ and $N-m$, then $H=S(O(m)\times O(N-m))$, with <identity component> $\operatorname{SO}(m)\times\operatorname{SO}(N-m)$.

For example a rank-one tensor $Q=ve_Ne_N^T$ preserves $O(N-1)$ embedded by $A\mapsto\operatorname{diag}(A,\det A)$. Its continuous unbroken <group> is $\operatorname{SO}(N-1)$, but it also allows reversal of the distinguished axis. Restricting to a traceless <symmetric tensor> imposes $\sum_i m_iq_i=0$ and otherwise leaves the same classification.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <complexification of a Lie algebra> gives $\mathfrak{so}(2n,\mathbb C)$ of type <Dn root system> and $\mathfrak{so}(2n+1,\mathbb C)$ of type <Bn root system>. For $D_n$, the <simple roots> $\alpha_i=e_i-e_{i+1}$ for $1\le i<n$ and $\alpha_n=e_{n-1}+e_n$ give a chain $1,\ldots,n-2$ with two terminal nodes $n-1,n$ joined to $n-2$. All edges are single. For $B_n$, use $\alpha_i=e_i-e_{i+1}$ for $i<n$ and $\alpha_n=e_n$. The <Bn Dynkin diagram> is a chain with a double final edge and an arrow pointing to the short node $n$.

\Image[2025-orthogonal-dynkin-diagrams.png]
{title=Dynkin diagrams of the odd and even orthogonal Lie algebras, with the final arrow pointing toward the short root}

The usual family diagrams apply to $D_n$ for $n\ge4$ and $B_n$ for $n\ge2$. The small cases are $D_3\cong A_3$, $D_2\cong A_1\oplus A_1$, $B_1\cong A_1$; $\mathfrak{so}(2)$ is one-dimensional and Abelian.

To fix <Casimir normalization for orthogonal vector representations>, use Hermitian <Lie algebra generators> $t_{ij}=i(E_{ij}-E_{ji})/\sqrt2$, so the defining <representation> has <Dynkin index> $c(\mathbf N)=1$. With long <roots> of squared length two, the <quadratic Casimir operator> has <Casimir eigenvalue>
$$
C_2(\lambda)=\frac12(\lambda,\lambda+2\rho)=\frac12\lambda^TG(\lambda+2\mathbf1),
$$
where $\rho$ is the <Weyl vector> and $\lambda$ in the final expression is the column of <Dynkin labels>. The defining <fundamental representation> has <highest weight> $(1,0,\ldots,0)$ in the stated family conventions.

For $D_n$, the row supplied in the source has sum $n-1$ and first entry one, so
$$
C_2(\mathbf{2n})=\frac12\{1+2(n-1)\}=\frac{2n-1}{2}.
$$
For $B_n$, the supplied row has sum $n-\tfrac12$, giving
$$
C_2(\mathbf{2n+1})=\frac12\{1+2(n-\tfrac12)\}=n.
$$
Taking the <trace> of $\sum_a t_at_a=C_2(R)I_R$ gives the relation between the <Dynkin index> and <Casimir eigenvalue>, $c(R)\dim\mathfrak g=C_2(R)\dim R$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{C_2(\mathbf N)=\frac{N-1}{2},\qquad c(\mathbf N)=1.}
$$
There is also a direct check: each coordinate appears in $N-1$ planes and $t_{ij}^2=(E_{ii}+E_{jj})/2$, so $\sum_{i<j}t_{ij}^2=(N-1)I/2$.

If the <Casimir operator> is instead defined by $C(\lambda)=(\lambda,\lambda+2\rho)$, every <Lie algebra generator> is larger by $\sqrt2$ and the corresponding answers are $C(\mathbf N)=N-1$ and $c(\mathbf N)=2$. These are the same <Lie algebra> in a different normalization. At the exceptional small ranks the family <Dynkin labels> of the vector change, but the direct <matrix> computation remains valid.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In the normalization $c(\mathbf8)=1$ of <2/c>, the <adjoint representation> of $D_4$ has <highest weight> $\lambda_{\mathrm{adj}}=(0,1,0,0)$, the <highest root>. The supplied <Gram matrix> has second row $(1,2,1,1)$, whose sum is five. Hence
$$
C_2(\mathrm{adj})=\frac12(2+2\cdot5)=6.
$$
The <adjoint representation> has the same dimension as the <Lie algebra>, so its <Dynkin index> is also $c(\mathrm{adj})=6$. The $k$ <Weyl fermions> form a <direct sum> of $k$ vector <representations>; additivity of the <trace> gives $c(r_W)=k$.

Substitution into the given one-loop <renormalization-group beta function> yields
$$
\boxed{\beta(g)=-\frac{g^3}{16\pi^2}\left(22-\frac{2k}{3}\right)
=-\frac{33-k}{24\pi^2}g^3.}
$$
For positive $g$, <asymptotic freedom> at this order requires a negative <beta function>, equivalently
$$
\boxed{k<33;\quad\text{for a nonnegative integer number of fermions, }k=0,1,\ldots,32.}
$$
Indeed, if $b_0=22-2k/3>0$, integrating $\mu\,dg/d\mu=-b_0g^3/(16\pi^2)$ gives
$$
\frac1{g^2(\mu)}=\frac1{g^2(\mu_0)}+\frac{b_0}{8\pi^2}\log\frac\mu{\mu_0},
$$
so the <gauge coupling> decreases towards zero as the energy scale increases. At $k=33$ the one-loop coefficient vanishes and the given equation cannot decide <asymptotic freedom>; $k>33$ has the opposite one-loop running.

With the alternative convention $C(\lambda)=\lambda^TG(\lambda+2\mathbf1)$ described in <2/c>, $c(\mathbf8)=2$, $c(\mathrm{adj})=12$, and the same source formula reads $\beta(g)=-(44-4k/3)g^3/(16\pi^2)$. Rescaling the <gauge coupling> together with the <Lie algebra generators> relates the conventions; the bound $k<33$ is identical.

