= Solution
{parent=a}

Adopt the <Minkowski metric> $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,+1)$ and the <Clifford algebra> convention $\{\gamma_\mu,\gamma_\nu\}=2\eta_{\mu\nu}I$. The source's <Pauli matrices> give
$$
\gamma_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\gamma_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
They satisfy $\gamma_0^2=-I$, $\gamma_1^2=I$ and $\gamma_0\gamma_1=-\gamma_1\gamma_0=-\sigma_3$, which verifies the required <Clifford algebra>. Raising the indices gives $\gamma^0=-\gamma_0$ and $\gamma^1=\gamma_1$.

Fix the sign of the <rapidity> $\theta$ by taking the <Lorentz boost>
$$
\begin{pmatrix}t'\\x'\end{pmatrix}
=\Lambda(\theta)\begin{pmatrix}t\\x\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\Lambda(\theta)=\begin{pmatrix}\cosh\theta&-\sinh\theta\\-\sinh\theta&\cosh\theta\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The corresponding <spinor representation of the Lorentz group> is
$$
S(\theta)=\exp\left(\frac\theta2\gamma^0\gamma^1\right)
=\exp\left(\frac\theta2\sigma_3\right)
=\operatorname{diag}(e^{\theta/2},e^{-\theta/2}).
$$
For example $S^{-1}\gamma^0S=\gamma^0\cosh\theta-\gamma^1\sinh\theta$ and $S^{-1}\gamma^1S=\gamma^1\cosh\theta-\gamma^0\sinh\theta$, establishing <Lorentz covariance> by $S^{-1}\gamma^\mu S=\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}\gamma^\nu$.

Thus the <Dirac spinor> transforms as $\psi'(x')=S(\theta)\psi(x)$, with infinitesimal law
$$
\boxed{\delta\psi=\frac\theta2\sigma_3\psi,\qquad
\delta\psi_+=\frac\theta2\psi_+,\qquad
\delta\psi_-=-\frac\theta2\psi_-.}
$$
These variations compare the fields at corresponding spacetime points. At a fixed coordinate one adds the usual orbital term $-\delta x^\mu\partial_\mu\psi$. Reversing the <rapidity> convention reverses both boost signs consistently. Infinitesimal transformations describe the <proper orthochronous Lorentz group>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose the <chirality> operator
$$
\Gamma=\gamma^0\gamma^1=\sigma_3,\qquad \Gamma^2=I,\qquad \{\Gamma,\gamma^\mu\}=0.
$$
A <Weyl spinor> is a <Dirac spinor> restricted to one <eigenspace> of $\Gamma$. The <chiral projectors> are $P_R=(I+\Gamma)/2$ and $P_L=(I-\Gamma)/2$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\psi_R=P_R\psi=\begin{pmatrix}\psi_+\\0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\psi_L=P_L\psi=\begin{pmatrix}0\\\psi_-\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The two independent <Weyl spinors> each contain one complex component. Since $[S(\theta),\Gamma]=0$, each <chirality> is preserved by every connected <Lorentz transformation>, with $\psi_R\mapsto e^{\theta/2}\psi_R$ and $\psi_L\mapsto e^{-\theta/2}\psi_L$. A spatial reflection exchanges the two <chiralities>.

The left/right names depend on the sign convention for $\Gamma$. Choosing $\gamma_0\gamma_1=-\sigma_3$ as the <chirality> operator exchanges the labels $L$ and $R$; the two invariant one-component <representations> are the same.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

All <gamma matrices> in this <basis> are real, and the <Lorentz transformation> $S(\theta)$ is real. Therefore <complex conjugation> commutes with the connected <spinor representation of the Lorentz group>. With a suitable phase convention, <charge conjugation> is simply $\psi^c=\psi^*$. The <Majorana spinor> condition $\psi^c=\psi$ becomes
$$
\boxed{\psi_M=\begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix},\qquad a^*=a,\quad b^*=b.}
$$
The reality constraint is preserved by <Lorentz transformations> because $(S\psi_M)^*=S\psi_M$. For fermion fields, real components mean self-conjugate <Grassmann variables> or self-adjoint field components, rather than ordinary commuting real numbers.

Moreover, $\Gamma=\sigma_3$ is real, so <charge conjugation> commutes with the <chiral projectors>. The <Majorana spinor> and <Weyl spinor> conditions can therefore be imposed simultaneously. The <Majorana-Weyl spinors> are
$$
\boxed{\psi_{MR}=\begin{pmatrix}a\\0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\psi_{ML}=\begin{pmatrix}0\\b\end{pmatrix},\qquad a^*=a,\ b^*=b.}
$$
The independent real component counts are
$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{spinor type}&\text{real components}\\\hline
\text{Dirac}&4\\
\text{Weyl, either chirality}&2\\
\text{Majorana}&2\\
\text{Majorana-Weyl, either chirality}&1
\end{array}.}
$$
These are component counts before imposing an equation of motion. In particular the simultaneous reality and <chirality> constraints are compatible in this spacetime dimension, unlike the familiar four-dimensional case.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Because the present $\gamma_0$ is anti-Hermitian, choose the <Dirac adjoint> with Hermitian intertwining matrix $A=i\gamma_0$:
$$
\bar\psi=\psi^\dagger A,\qquad A=i\gamma_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
This convention makes the <Dirac scalar bilinear> $\bar\psi\psi$ Hermitian. For real $m$, a <Lorentz invariant> <mass term> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L_m=-m\bar\psi\psi
=im\left(\psi_+^*\psi_- -\psi_-^*\psi_+\right).}
$$
To check infinitesimal <Lorentz invariance>, put $K=\sigma_3/2$. Then $K^\dagger=K$ and $K^\dagger A+AK=\{K,A\}=0$. Using <3/a>,
$$
\delta\mathcal L_m=-m\theta\psi^\dagger(K^\dagger A+AK)\psi=0.
$$
Equivalently, $\delta(\psi_+^*\psi_-)=\theta\psi_+^*\psi_-/2-\theta\psi_+^*\psi_-/2=0$, and similarly for its <complex conjugate>. Exponentiation gives $S^\dagger AS=A$ and therefore $\bar\psi'(x')=\bar\psi(x)S^{-1}$.

At fixed coordinates $\mathcal L_m$ has only the orbital variation of a <Lorentz scalar>; the spacetime measure is invariant under the connected <Lorentz group>, so the integrated <action> is invariant. The <mass term> pairs the two opposite <chiralities>; retaining only one <Weyl spinor> removes this Dirac mass. An equivalent spinor phase convention gives a real multiple of $\psi_+^*\psi_-+\psi_-^*\psi_+$ instead.

